  "content": "2026–2027 El Niño event - Wikipedia\n[Jump to content](#bodyContent)\n[\n](\/wiki\/Main_Page)\n[\nSearch\n](\/wiki\/Special:Search)\nSearch\n[ Donate ](https:\/\/donate.wikimedia.org\/?wmf_source=donate&wmf_medium=sidebar&wmf_campaign=en.wikipedia.org&uselang=en)\n[ Create account ](\/w\/index.php?title=Special:CreateAccount&returnto=2026%E2%80%932027+El+Ni%C3%B1o+event)\n[ Log in ](\/w\/index.php?title=Special:UserLogin&returnto=2026%E2%80%932027+El+Ni%C3%B1o+event)\nPersonal tools\n[\nDonate ](https:\/\/donate.wikimedia.org\/?wmf_source=donate&wmf_medium=sidebar&wmf_campaign=en.wikipedia.org&uselang=en)\n[\nCreate account ](\/w\/index.php?title=Special:CreateAccount&returnto=2026%E2%80%932027+El+Ni%C3%B1o+event)\n[\nLog in ](\/w\/index.php?title=Special:UserLogin&returnto=2026%E2%80%932027+El+Ni%C3%B1o+event)\n#   2026–2027 El Niño event\n4 languages\n[ Español ](https:\/\/es.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Evento_de_El_Ni%C3%B1o_de_2026-2027)  [ فارسی ](https:\/\/fa.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/%D8%B1%D9%88%DB%8C%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%AF_%D8%A7%D9%84_%D9%86%DB%8C%D9%86%D9%88_%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B2%DB%B7%E2%80%93%DB%B2%DB%B0%DB%B2%DB%B6)  [ Français ](https:\/\/fr.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Ni%C3%B1o_de_2026-2027)  [ Português ](https:\/\/pt.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Ni%C3%B1o_(2026%E2%80%93presente))\n[Edit links](https:\/\/www.wikidata.org\/wiki\/Special:EntityPage\/Q140160497#sitelinks-wikipedia)\nFrom Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia\nMeteorological event\n2026–2027 El Niño event\n[ ](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/File:El_Niño_warming_pattern_in_the_Pacific_Ocean_(Copernicus_2026-08-26).webp)  Data visualisation of sea surface temperature anomalies in the Pacific Ocean in August 2026, during the 2026–2027 El Niño event       Meteorological history     Formed  June 2026\nOverall effects    Areas   affected  The Pacific Ocean, Southern Hemisphere, and Pacific regions of the Northern Hemisphere\nThe  2026–2027 El Niño  is the ongoing [El Niño](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Niño) event, with its onset declared on 2 June 2026 by the [World Meteorological Organization](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/World_Meteorological_Organization) (WMO). [  [ 1 ]  ](#cite_note-auto-1)  It is the strongest El Niño ever measured for its time of year, and the [World Meteorological Organization](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/World_Meteorological_Organization) has reported that it will likely be the strongest [El Niño](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Niño) on record, with it already being classified as a \"very strong El Niño\", also known as a [Super El Niño](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Super_El_Niño_events). The 2026–2027 El Niño is expected to peak in November or December and last through early 2027. [  [ 2 ]  ](#cite_note-2)\n## Background\n[ [ edit ](\/w\/index.php?title=2026%E2%80%932027_El_Ni%C3%B1o_event&action=edit&section=1) ]\n[ ](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/File:NOAA_CPC_ENSO_probabilities_May_2026.png) Official NOAA CPC ENSO probabilities as of May 2026\n[ ](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/File:2026–27_El_Niño_loop_(slow).gif) An animation of the developing [El Niño](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/ENSO) from June 1 to September 21\nEl Niño is defined as when the Relative Ocean Niño Index (RONI) in the Niño 3.4 region is greater than 0.5 °C for five three-month averaged periods in a row.\nA weak [La Niña](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Niño–Southern_Oscillation) event evolved to ENSO-neutral in early 2026, but was rapidly transitioning to El Niño by May, according to Columbia University's [Climate School.](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Columbia_Climate_School) [  [ 3 ]  ](#cite_note-Columbia_Climate_School-3)  [  [ 4 ]  ](#cite_note-4)  Computer modeling in late May by the US [National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/National_Oceanic_and_Atmospheric_Administration) (NOAA) suggested the development of an [El Niño](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Niño) event was likely by the July through September peak of the [Pacific hurricane season](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Pacific_hurricane). [  [ 5 ]  ](#cite_note-cpc_ncep-5)  The [Center for Climate Systems Research](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Center_for_Climate_Systems_Research) (CCSR) and the [International Research Institute for Climate and Society](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/International_Research_Institute_for_Climate_and_Society) (IRI) ENSO plume forecast gave a 98% probability of El Niño forming and a 97%-98% probability of continuing through the end of the year, a range Columbia called \"remarkably high and narrow\". [  [ 3 ]  ](#cite_note-Columbia_Climate_School-3)\nOn 2 June, the United Nations [World Meteorological Organization](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/World_Meteorological_Organization) confirmed \"that El Niño conditions are developing in the tropical Pacific and are expected to influence weather and climate patterns around the world in the months ahead.\" [  [ 1 ]  ](#cite_note-auto-1)  [  [ 6 ]  ](#cite_note-6)  The [European Centre for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/European_Centre_for_Medium-Range_Weather_Forecasts) released a forecast of a central equatorial [Pacific Ocean](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Pacific_Ocean) temperature anomaly of  3–4     °C (5.4–7.2     °F) , which would make it the strongest on record. [  [ 7 ]  ](#cite_note-7)  [  [ 8 ]  ](#cite_note-8)\nThe global average [sea surface temperature](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Sea_surface_temperature) for June reached an all-time record high of  21.1     °C (70.0     °F) . [  [ 9 ]  ](#cite_note-9)  That month, the [National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/National_Oceanic_and_Atmospheric_Administration) (NOAA) confirmed the emergence of a strong El Niño, giving it a 63% chance of becoming intense enough by late fall and early winter to be one of the strongest on record. [  [ 10 ]  ](#cite_note-10)  On 8 October, NOAA stated that there was a greater than 83% chance that it would become the strongest El Niño on record by October-December. [  [ 11 ]  ](#cite_note-11)\n## Timeline\n[ [ edit ](\/w\/index.php?title=2026%E2%80%932027_El_Ni%C3%B1o_event&action=edit&section=2) ]\nThe examples and perspectives in this section  deal primarily with the United States and do not represent a [worldwide view](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Wikipedia:WikiProject_Countering_systemic_bias) of the subject .  You may [improve this section](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Special:EditPage\/2026–2027_El_Niño_event), discuss the issue on the [talk page](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Talk:2026–2027_El_Niño_event), or create a new section, as appropriate.     ( October 2026 )     ( [Learn how and when to remove this message](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Help:Maintenance_template_removal) )\n### April\n[ [ edit ](\/w\/index.php?title=2026%E2%80%932027_El_Ni%C3%B1o_event&action=edit&section=3) ]\n20 April – [Columbia University](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Columbia_University)'s [Climate School](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Columbia_Climate_School) reports that the weak [La Niña](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Niño–Southern_Oscillation) event had transitioned into an ENSO-neutral state. [  [ 3 ]  ](#cite_note-Columbia_Climate_School-3)\n### May\n[ [ edit ](\/w\/index.php?title=2026%E2%80%932027_El_Ni%C3%B1o_event&action=edit&section=4) ]\n21 May – Computer modeling by the US [National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/National_Oceanic_and_Atmospheric_Administration) (NOAA) suggested the development of an [El Niño](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/El_Niño) event was likely by the July through September peak of the [Pacific hurricane season](https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/Pacific_hurricane). [  [ 5 ]  ](#cite_note-cpc_ncep-5)\n### June\n[ [ edit ](\/w\/index.php?title=2026%E2%80%932027_El_Ni%C3%B1o_event&action=edit&section=5) ]\nThe northern hemisphere summer temperatures in the east-central Pacific are among the highest on record, with NOAA predicting them to exceed those of past powerful El Niños. [  [ 12 ]  ](#cite_note-12)\n2 June  \n\n... [Truncated 67966 additional characters]"
